    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9279A\n\nSPONSOR: GIANARIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to the operation of\ngene synthesis providers and manufacturers of gene synthesis equipment\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\n\"To amend the public health law, in relation to the operation of gene\nsynthesis providers and manufacturers of gene synthesis equipment\"\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Provides a short title: \"Bioterrorism Prevention Act.\"\n\nSection 2: Amends the public health law by adding article 32-B which\nrequires any gene synthesis provider or manufacturers of gene synthesis\nequipment in the state to operate in accordance with rules and regu-\nlations issued by the Department of Health.\n\nSection 3: Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nGene synthesis describes the synthetic biology process utilized to\ncreate artificial genes. This is most commonly done within a laboratory\nsetting, and recently, has been used widespread by large biotech corpo-\nrations. Recent advancements in gene synthesis technology present both\ngreat opportunities as well as great risks.  New York currently lacks\neven basic regulations to ensure gene synthesis technologies do not put\nbioweapons in the hands of terrorists. For example, as the law currently\nstands, gene synthesis producers do not even need to screen orders\nagainst federal databases of dangerous individuals who pose a known\nthreat to the state. By requiring gene synthesis providers and manufac-\nturers of gene synthesis equipment to act in accordance with Department\nof Health regulations, developed in consideration of the International\nGene Synthesis Consortium protocols, this bill will address the poten-\ntial catastrophic threat posed by New York's lack of gene synthesis\nsafety controls.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
